If a patient takes a short-acting-full-agonist-opioid and the short-acting-full-agonist-opioid has a last-dose and a clinician plans a first-buprenorphine-dose for the patient and a buprenorphine-transition-wait has a duration and the duration includes at least 8 hours then the clinician should use the buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose and the patient should use the buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose.
If a patient takes a long-acting-full-agonist-opioid and the long-acting-full-agonist-opioid has a last-dose and a clinician plans a first-buprenorphine-dose for the patient and a longer-buprenorphine-transition-wait has a duration then the clinician should use the longer-buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose and the patient should use the longer-buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose.
If a patient takes an ER-LA-full-agonist-opioid and the ER-LA-full-agonist-opioid has a last-dose and a clinician plans a first-buprenorphine-dose for the patient and a buprenorphine-transition-wait has a duration and the duration includes at least 12 hours then the clinician should use the buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose and the patient should use the buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose.
If a patient receives a methadone-treatment and the methadone-treatment has a last-dose and a clinician plans a first-buprenorphine-dose for the patient and a longer-buprenorphine-transition-wait has a duration then the clinician should use the longer-buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose and the patient should use the longer-buprenorphine-transition-wait after the last-dose before the first-buprenorphine-dose.
